---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import GiveStrip from '../components/GiveStrip.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
---
<Base color="gold" title="How It Works" description="Who we serve, what we pay for, how we choose recipients, and how scholarship money is paid.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p>
        Our scholarships go to people who have already committed to lead: high schoolers headed into ROTC or service academy prep, and enlisted service members finishing the degree they need to commission. We fund enough to change where they can go to school, and we pay the school directly. Here is who qualifies, what we pay for, how we choose, and how the money moves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <GiveStrip />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container cols cols-top">
      <div>
        <h2>Who we serve</h2>
        <p>U.S. citizens or permanent residents who show a genuine intent to become military officers, and who are either:</p>
        <ul class="bars">
          <li>High school juniors or seniors headed to a college with ROTC, or in service academy prep; or</li>
          <li>Active-duty enlisted service members of any branch pursuing the degree they need to commission, through programs such as Green to Gold, STA-21 or OCS/OTS.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>What we pay for</h2>
        <ul class="bars">
          <li>Tuition and mandatory fees</li>
          <li>Room and board</li>
          <li>Books and required materials</li>
          <li>Testing and certification fees, such as standardized tests</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Every path to a commission, and where we come in</h2>
        <p>
          There's more than one way to become an officer, and most of them leave someone holding the bill. Some candidates already have a path and are short on money. Others hit the money problem before they ever reach a path. Here's where a scholarship changes the story on each.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="beats">
        <div>
          <h3>ROTC, with a scholarship</h3>
          <p>
            A scholarship cadet already has a path and an award. But awards are fixed dollar amounts, colleges raise prices every year, and the cadet stipend ceiling was last set by Congress in FY2001. Many awards don't cover room and board at all. At a top university, the award covers a fraction and the family covers the rest. We fund what the award doesn't.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>ROTC, on your own dime</h3>
          <p>
            Many cadets have a partial scholarship or none at all. They wear the uniform, show up for physical training before dawn and leadership labs after class, and still pay for school like everyone else, with little time left for a job. They've already proven the commitment. They're exactly who we're for.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>A commission after you graduate</h3>
          <p>
            Some programs give you a path to a commission (train in the summers now, commission when you graduate) but leave most of the cost of school to you. Officer Candidate School and Officer Training School go further: you need a finished bachelor's degree before you can start, paid for on your own. The path is there. The degree is the hurdle, and that's what we help pay for.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Service academy prep</h3>
          <p>
            The service academies themselves are fully funded. The road to them isn't. Prep programs, testing and the time in between all cost money, and a strong candidate on a tight budget may not be able to wait. We help cover the cost of getting ready, so the decision about an appointment is made on merit.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Enlisted, going for a commission</h3>
          <p>
            Green to Gold, STA-21 and similar programs open the door from the enlisted ranks to a commission, but the degree still has to be paid for. Military Tuition Assistance went from $4,000 to $4,500 a year while public tuition rose 164%. GI Bill benefits help, but they have limits, especially at a private university. On enlisted pay, the gap is real. We close it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Growing up in an enlisted family</h3>
          <p>
            Some of the strongest candidates grew up watching a parent serve. They know what leadership in uniform looks like, and they want to lead. But a family living on enlisted pay faces the same tuition bill as everyone else, and transferred GI Bill benefits have limits too. They apply as high school candidates like anyone else, and we want them in the room.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="coda">Whatever the path, we ask the same question: does this scholarship change where you can go? If it does, that's who we fund.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>How we choose</h2>
        <p>
          A competitive, merit-based review by an independent selection committee of board members and volunteers with relevant backgrounds: educators, veterans and financial professionals. Committee members recuse themselves from any applicant they know personally.
        </p>
        <p>Financial need is one of the criteria, along with:</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="bars bars-center">
        <li>Academic achievement and potential</li>
        <li>Commitment to military service</li>
        <li>Leadership</li>
        <li>Recommendations</li>
        <li>A personal statement</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container cols">
      <div>
        <h2>How the money is paid</h2>
        <ul class="bars">
          <li>Money goes directly to the school, never to the individual.</li>
          <li>Enrollment and academic progress are verified before every payment.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Recipients keep satisfactory academic progress, stay on the officer path, send an annual progress report, and tell us if their plans change.</p>
      </div>
      <Photo hint="A campus or classroom, ideally a recipient's school" ratio="4 / 3" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Beyond scholarships</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="bars bars-center">
        <li>Information on officer career pathways</li>
        <li>Guidance on scholarship and commissioning applications</li>
        <li>Workshops for prospective applicants</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block block-center c-orange">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Applications are not yet open</h2>
      <p>We will publish the application form and timeline once the first scholarship cycle is funded.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/apply" class="btn">Get notified</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
